@end quotation

@node	4.3.3, 4.4, 4.2, 4.3
@subsection Implementing the Search Evaluator

The evaluation of an ordinary guest expression may return a value, may never
terminate, or may signal a typed guest error.  In the search language of
@ref{4.3.1} the evaluation of an expression may in addition result in the
discovery of a dead end, in which case evaluation must backtrack to a previous
choice point.  The interpretation of the search language is complicated by
this extra case.

The @code{SearchModule} is a direct evaluator for the admitted guest syntax.
Its recursive evaluator threads two continuations: success receives the value
and the failure continuation for another answer; failure resumes the latest
untried choice.  Unlike the preceding analyzed evaluator, it does not first
compile expressions to execution closures.

@subsubheading Execution procedures and continuations

Recall that the execution procedures for the ordinary evaluator take one
argument: the environment of execution.  The execution procedures in the
search evaluator take three arguments: the environment, and two procedures
called @newterm{continuation procedures}.  The evaluation of an expression
will finish by calling the @newterm{success continuation} with the value it
produced and the @newterm{failure continuation} that resumes the search, or
by calling the failure continuation at once if the evaluation results in the
discovery of a dead end.  Constructing appropriate success continuations, and
chaining failures, is the mechanism by which the nondeterministic evaluator
implements backtracking.  In the engine these are plain function values over
guest values:

@example kotlin
private typealias Ok = (GValue, Fail) -> Unit

private typealias Fail = () -> Unit
@end example

It is the job of the success continuation to receive a value and the failure
continuation that resumes the search, and proceed with the computation.  If
the use of that value leads to a dead end, the failure continuation runs; the
pending choices it closes over are exactly the places the search can resume.

It is the job of a failure to try another branch of the nondeterministic
process.  The essence of the nondeterministic language is in the fact that
expressions may represent choices among alternatives.  The evaluation of such
an expression must proceed with one of the indicated alternative choices, even
though it is not known in advance which choices will lead to acceptable
results.  To deal with this, the evaluator picks one of the alternatives and
passes this value to the success continuation, closing over the remaining
alternatives in the failure continuation.

A failure is called during evaluation when a user program explicitly rejects
the current line of attack (for example, a call to @code{demand} may result in
execution of @code{choose()}, an expression that always fails---see
@ref{4.3.1}).  Calling the failure continuation resumes the deepest pending
choice.  If there are no more alternatives to be considered at that choice,
the failure propagates to the choice made by an earlier choice point, and so
on.  One @code{SearchModule.run} explores every alternative this way: the
output accumulates the effects of all attempts in search order, the first
success becomes the run's value, and the choice count reports one choice per
entered alternative.

In addition, if an assignment occurs on a branch, a later failure must undo
that branch's writes before another alternative runs.  At each choice the
engine snapshots visible cells and marks its mutation log; on backtracking it
restores mutations to that mark and then replays writes enclosed by
@code{setPermanent} (@ref{Exercise 4.51}).

In summary, failures are constructed by

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{choose} expressions---an exhausted choice fails outright, and an
empty @code{choose()} fails immediately;

@item
the top-level run---when the last pending choice is exhausted, the search
ends with no further answers.

@end itemize

@noindent
Failures are initiated only when a dead end is encountered.  This occurs

@itemize @bullet

@item
if the user program executes @code{choose()};

@item
if a @code{demand} test is false.

@end itemize

@noindent
Failures are also processed during backtracking:

@itemize @bullet

@item
When the engine resumes a pending choice, it restores the cell snapshot and
mutation mark, so assignments performed inside an abandoned branch are undone
before the branch's alternative runs.

@item
When the failure continuation for a @code{choose} runs out of choices, the
failure propagates back to the previous choice point or to the top level.

@end itemize

@subsubheading Structure of the evaluator

The syntax- and data-representation procedures for the search evaluator
use the same admitted guest AST as the evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}; the direct
CPS evaluator recognizes
the search intrinsics (@code{choose}, @code{chooseRandom}, @code{demand},
@code{setPermanent}, @code{ifFail}) where the book's tagged-list recognition
checked for @code{choose}.@footnote{Our search programs use local functions
(see @ref{Exercise 4.22}), which the guest language admits directly.}

@example kotlin
private val searchIntrinsics: Set<String> =
    setOf("choose",
        "chooseRandom",
        "demand",
        "setPermanent",
        "ifFail",
        "seededRandom")

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun evalIntrinsic(name: String,
    expression: Call,
    env: Env,
    ok: Ok, fail: Fail) @{
    when (name) @{
        "choose" -> evalChoose(expression,
            env,
            ok,
            fail,
            randomized = false)
        "chooseRandom" -> evalChoose(expression,
            env,
            ok,
            fail,
            randomized = true)
        "demand" -> evalDemand(expression,
            env,
            ok,
            fail)
        "setPermanent" -> evalSetPermanent(expression,
            env,
            ok,
            fail)
        "ifFail" -> evalIfFail(expression,
            env,
            ok,
            fail)
        else -> evalSeededRandom(expression,
            env,
            ok,
            fail)
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The extension names follow the admitted surface exactly: @code{chooseRandom},
@code{setPermanent}, @code{ifFail}, and @code{seededRandom}.

The public entry point admits guest source in @code{Mode.SEARCH}, then
explores all alternatives:

@example kotlin
public fun run(source: String): Either<AdmissionError,
    SearchRun> =
    either @{ execute(Admission.admitOrRaise(source,
        Mode.SEARCH)) @}
@end example

@noindent
The continuation aliases are the engine's actual private types.  A
@code{SearchRun} carries the run observation and the number of alternatives
entered; effects from failed branches remain in the output, and
@code{mainValue} is the first successful result or null when search exhausts.

@subsubheading Simple expressions

The direct evaluator delivers a literal or a variable value to the success
continuation together with the failure continuation that remains available:

@example kotlin
is Literal -> ok(literalValue(expression), fail)
is Name -> ok(readName(expression.text,
        expression,
    env),
    fail)
@end example

@noindent
A variable read checks the lexical environment and then the global frame;
an unbound or unassigned read raises a guest error, not a search failure:

@example kotlin
private fun readName(name: String,
    at: Expression,
    env: Env): GValue @{
    val cell = env.lookup(name) ?: globals.lookup(name)
        ?: r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRead(at.span))
    val value = cell.value
    if (value is GValue.VUnassigned)
        r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRead(at.span))
    return value
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Conditionals and sequences

A conditional evaluates its Boolean test, then follows only the selected
branch.  A statement sequence threads the failure continuation from one
statement to the next, so a later failed choice resumes an earlier one:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun evalIf(expression: If,
    env: Env,
    ok: Ok,
    fail: Fail) @{
    eval(expression.condition, env, @{ condition, _ ->
        val branch = if (Primitives.truth(condition,
            expression.condition.span)) @{
            expression.yes
        @} else @{
            expression.no
        @}
        if (branch != null) eval(branch, env, ok, fail)
        else ok(GValue.VUnit, fail)
    @}, fail)
@}

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun runStatements(statements: List<Statement>,
    env: Env,
    ok: Ok,
    fail: Fail) @{
    fun step(index: Int, currentFail: Fail) @{
        if (index == statements.size) @{
            ok(GValue.VUnit, currentFail)
            return
        @}
        execStatement(statements[index], env,
            @{ _,
                more -> step(index + 1,
                more) @},
                currentFail)
    @}
    step(0, fail)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Definitions and assignments

Definitions evaluate their initializer and install its value in the current
environment.  An assignment evaluates its right side first, then writes to
the resolved target.  Search rollback is handled by the mutation log at
choice points; @code{setPermanent} records writes for replay.  A missing
binding raises @code{GuestError.UnassignedRead}, not failure:

@example kotlin
is Assignment -> eval(statement.value,
    env,
    @{ value,
    more ->
    writeAssignment(statement, value, env)
    ok(GValue.VUnit, more)
@}, fail)

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun writeAssignment(
    statement: Assignment,
    value: GValue,
    env: Env,
) @{
    val target = statement.target
    if (target is Name) @{
        val cell = env.lookup(target.text)
            ?: globals.lookup(target.text)
            ?: r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRead(target.span))
        if (permanentDepth > 0) permanent.add(cell to value)
        cell.value = value
        return
    @}
    if (target is Member) @{
        val receiver =
            evalDirect(target.receiver,
                env) as? GValue.VObject
                ?: r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRead(target.span))
        val before = FieldsState(
            receiver,
            LinkedHashMap(receiver.fields),
        )
        receiver.fields[target.name] = value
        recordWrite(
            before,
            FieldsState(receiver,
                LinkedHashMap(receiver.fields)),
        )
        return
    @}
    if (target is Index) @{
        val receiver = evalDirect(target.receiver, env)
        val index = evalDirect(target.index, env)
        val before = storeState(receiver,
            statement.span)
        Primitives.writeIndex(receiver,
            index,
            value,
            statement.span)
        recordWrite(before,
                storeState(receiver,
            statement.span))
        return
    @}
    r.raise(GuestError.UnassignedRead(statement.span))
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Procedure applications

Applications first dispatch search intrinsics.  Other name calls resolve a
bound guest function, a constructor, or a primitive; member calls resolve a
guest method or a primitive operation.  Arguments are evaluated left to right:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun evalCall(
    expression: Call,
    env: Env,
    ok: Ok,
    fail: Fail,
) @{
    val callee = expression.callee
    if (callee is Name &&
        callee.text in searchIntrinsics) @{
        evalIntrinsic(callee.text,
            expression,
            env,
            ok,
            fail)
        return
    @}
    if (callee is Name) @{
        val bound = env.lookup(callee.text)
            ?: globals.lookup(callee.text)
        if (bound != null &&
            bound.value !is GValue.VUnassigned) @{
            evalArguments(expression,
                env,
                @{ arguments,
                more ->
                applyFunction(bound.value,
                    arguments,
                    ok,
                    more)
            @}, fail)
            return
        @}
        val shape = classTable[callee.text]
        if (shape != null) @{
            evalArguments(expression,
                env,
                @{ arguments,
                more ->
                val fields = linkedMapOf<String,
                    GValue>()
                for (
                    (property,
                        value) in shape.properties.zip(arguments)
                ) @{
                    fields[property.name] = value
                @}
                ok(
                    GValue.VObject(
                        shape.name,
                        structural = shape.data,
                        fields = fields,
                    ),
                    more,
                )
            @}, fail)
            return
        @}
        evalArguments(expression,
            env,
            @{ arguments,
            more ->
            ok(
                Primitives.call(
                    callee.text,
                    arguments,
                    sink,
                    expression.span,
                ),
                more,
            )
        @}, fail)
        return
    @}
    if (callee is Member) @{
        eval(callee.receiver, env, @{ receiver, _ ->
            evalArguments(expression,
                env,
                @{ arguments,
                more ->
                val shape = (receiver as? GValue.VObject)
                    ?.let @{ classTable[it.className] @}
                val method = shape?.methods?.get(callee.name)
                if (method != null) @{
                    applyFunction(
                        functionValue(method, globals),
                        arguments,
                        ok,
                        more,
                    )
                @} else @{
                    memberCallCps(
                        receiver,
                        callee.name,
                        arguments,
                        ok,
                        more,
                        expression.span,
                    )
                @}
            @}, fail)
        @}, fail)
        return
    @}
    eval(callee, env, @{ fn, _ ->
        evalArguments(expression,
            env,
            @{ arguments,
            more ->
            applyFunction(fn, arguments, ok, more)
        @}, fail)
    @}, fail)
@}

context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun applyFunction(
    fn: GValue,
    arguments: List<GValue>,
    ok: Ok,
    fail: Fail,
) @{
    val closure = closures[fn]
    if (closure != null) @{
        runClosureStreaming(closure,
            arguments,
            ok,
            fail)
        return
    @}
    if (fn !is GValue.VFunction) @{
        ok(Primitives.invoke(fn,
                arguments,
            NO_POSITION),
            fail)
        return
    @}
    ok(fn.apply(r, arguments), fail)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Evaluating @code{choose} expressions

The @code{choose} intrinsic evaluates alternatives one at a time.  At a
choice point, the engine snapshots visible cells and marks the mutation log.
Failure restores that state before entering the next alternative; each
entered alternative increments the run's choice count.  An empty choice
fails immediately:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<GuestError>)
private fun evalChoose(
    expression: Call,
    env: Env,
    ok: Ok,
    fail: Fail,
    randomized: Boolean,
) @{
    val alternatives = expression.arguments.map @{ it.value @}
    if (alternatives.isEmpty()) @{
        fail()
        return
    @}
    val order = if (randomized) @{
        val generator = random ?: GValue.VRandom(0L).also @{ random = it
            @}
        shuffledIndices(alternatives.size, generator)
    @} else alternatives.indices.toList()
    val snapshot = snapshotCells(env)
    val mark = mutations.size
    fun tryAt(position: Int) @{
        if (position == order.size) @{
            fail()
            return
        @}
        restoreMutated(mark)
        restoreCells(snapshot)
        replayPermanent()
        state.choices++
        eval(alternatives[order[position]], env,
            ok) @{ tryAt(position + 1) @}
    @}
    tryAt(0)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Driver loop

The public driver admits source in search mode, explores it to exhaustion,
and returns the run result plus the choice count.  Effects are captured in
program order; the first successful value is retained, while later failures
continue the search:

@example kotlin
public fun run(source: String): Either<AdmissionError,
    SearchRun> =
    either @{ execute(Admission.admitOrRaise(source,
        Mode.SEARCH)) @}

private fun execute(checked: CheckedProgram): SearchRun @{
    val sink = OutputSink()
    val outcome: Either<GuestError, SearchState> =
        either @{ SearchEvaluator(checked,
            sink).explore() @}
    return outcome.fold(
        @{ error -> SearchRun(RunResult(sink.contents(),
                error,
            null),
            0L) @},
        @{ state -> SearchRun(
            RunResult(sink.contents(),
                null,
                state.firstAnswer),
            state.choices,
        ) @},
    )
@}
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.50}Exercise 4.50:} The search mode admits the
@code{chooseRandom} intrinsic, which is like @code{choose} except that it
searches alternatives in the order of the program's seeded generator
(@code{seededRandom}) rather than from left to right.  Show how this can help
with Alyssa's problem in @ref{Exercise 4.49}.  A run that uses
@code{chooseRandom} is reproducible from its seed.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.51}Exercise 4.51:} The search mode admits a
@code{setPermanent} block whose writes are not undone upon failure.  For
example, we can choose two distinct elements from a list and count the number
of trials required to make a successful choice as follows:

@example kotlin
var count: Long = 0L

fun elementOf(items: List<String>): String @{
    demand(!items.isEmpty())
    val first = items.get(0)
    if (items.size == 1) @{
        return first
    @}
    return choose(first, elementOf(items.drop(1)))
@}

fun main() @{
    val x = elementOf(listOf("a", "b", "c"))
    val y = elementOf(listOf("a", "b", "c"))
    setPermanent @{ count = count + 1L @}
    demand(x != y)
    println("$x $y $count")
@}
@end example

@noindent
The run prints @code{a b 2} for the first success and @code{a c 3} for the
next: the counter survives backtracking, so it counts trials, not just the
choices behind one answer.

What values would have been displayed if we had used an ordinary assignment
here rather than @code{setPermanent}?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.52}Exercise 4.52:} The search mode admits
@code{ifFail}, which takes two zero-argument blocks.  It evaluates the first
block and returns its answers; if that block exhausts its choices, it evaluates
the second block.  For example:

@example kotlin
fun elementOfLong(items: List<Long>): Long @{
    demand(!items.isEmpty())
    val first = items.get(0)
    val rest = items.drop(1)
    if (rest.isEmpty()) return first
    return choose(first, elementOfLong(rest))
@}
@end example

@example kotlin
fun firstEven(items: List<Long>): String @{
    return ifFail(
        @{
            val x = elementOfLong(items)
            demand(x % 2L == 0L)
            "$x"
        @},
        @{ "all-odd" @},
    )
@}

fun main() @{
    println(firstEven(listOf(1L, 3L, 5L)))
    println(firstEven(listOf(1L, 3L, 5L, 8L)))
@}
// all-odd
// 8
@end example
@end quotation

